<Layouts.public page_title={@page_title}>
  <.updated updated_on={@updated_on} />

  <p class="legal-lede">
    {@service_name} is self-hosted: this instance is run by {@operator}, who
    decides what happens to the data in it. This page describes what the
    software collects, why, and how long it keeps it.
  </p>

  <h2>What the portal stores</h2>

  <h3>Your account</h3>
  <p>
    An email address and a display name, supplied by your OIDC identity provider
    or configured for the local bootstrap account. The local bootstrap account
    also stores a password hash, which is verified at sign-in. OIDC sign-in is
    delegated to your identity provider.
  </p>

  <h3>Your session</h3>
  <p>
    One cookie, signed and encrypted, holding a token that identifies you to the
    server. It is marked <code>HttpOnly</code> and <code>SameSite=Lax</code>, and
    on an HTTPS deployment it is marked <code>Secure</code> so it is never sent
    over plain HTTP. Signing out deletes it. Your colour-theme choice is kept in
    your browser's local storage and never reaches the server.
  </p>

  <h3>Security signals</h3>
  <p>
    To stop password spraying and abusive traffic, the server counts recent
    requests per client address and recent failed sign-ins per account. Those
    counters hold an address, a timestamp and an account identifier, live in
    memory only, and are lost entirely when the service restarts. Rate-limit
    timestamps and inactive client addresses are retained for up to 24 hours
    plus the five-minute cleanup interval. Failed sign-ins are pruned to the
    configured counting window (15 minutes by default), with cleanup every five
    minutes; active lockouts last for the configured duration (15 minutes by
    default). Denied attempts are written to the server log.
  </p>

  <h3>Your work</h3>
  <p>
    Diagrams and their share cards, pull request and issue records fetched from
    GitHub when your operator has connected a token, work-queue messages,
    no-mistakes run history, and the inbox messages the <code>fm-steer</code>
    CLI puts and acknowledges. The portal keeps a version history of these
    records so a crew can see what changed and when.
  </p>

  <h3>Integration credentials</h3>
  <p>
    Credentials a tenant stores — Discord keys, GitHub tokens, provider API keys
    — are encrypted with an authenticated cipher before they are written to the
    database, and are never returned in plaintext over HTTP or MCP. Secret values
    are also filtered out of request and event logs.
  </p>

  <h3>Discord interactions</h3>
  <p>
    When a tenant connects Discord, each interaction Discord sends carries the
    invoking user's Discord ID, the guild and channel it came from, and the
    command and its options. The portal verifies the signature, works out which
    tenant the request belongs to, and publishes the payload onto that tenant's
    message stream for its crew to act on. It does not build a profile of Discord
    users, and it does not read message history — Discord only sends the commands
    aimed at the application.
  </p>

  <h2>Why</h2>
  <p>
    Account and work data are processed to provide the service you asked for.
    Security signals are processed for the legitimate interest of keeping the
    service available and other people's accounts safe. There is no other
    purpose: no advertising, no profiling, no analytics product, and no sale or
    rental of anything on this page to anyone.
  </p>

  <h2>Who else sees it</h2>
  <p>
    Data stays on infrastructure {@operator} controls — the application's
    database and message broker. Beyond that, it reaches a third party only
    where you connect one:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      your OIDC identity provider, which authenticates you and tells the portal your email and name;
    </li>
    <li>
      GitHub, when your operator configures a token, so the portal can read the pull requests and issues you asked it to track;
    </li>
    <li>Discord, when a tenant connects it, for the interactions described above;</li>
    <li>
      the network provider in front of the public hostname, which terminates TLS
      and sees request metadata as any CDN or load balancer does.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    A diagram share link requires a signed-in reader in the tenant that owns
    the diagram. Signed-out requests to <code>/d/:id</code> return 404; a link
    alone grants no access. The PNG preview at <code>/d/:id/card.png</code>
    has a separate exception: link-preview crawlers can fetch an available card
    without signing in. Because this relies on a self-reported
    <code>User-Agent</code>, treat the card image as effectively public: anyone
    with the link can read anything legible in it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Apart from these disclosures, data is disclosed only where the law requires it.
  </p>

  <h2>Cookies</h2>
  <p>
    One strictly necessary session cookie, described above. No analytics
    cookies, no advertising cookies, no third-party trackers, and no consent
    banner, because there is nothing to consent to.
  </p>

  <h2>How long it is kept</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Session cookie: until you sign out, or it expires.</li>
    <li>
      Rate-limit counters: up to 24 hours plus the five-minute cleanup interval,
      in memory and cleared on restart.
    </li>
    <li>
      Failed sign-in counters: the configured counting window (15 minutes by
      default) plus up to five minutes for cleanup. Active lockouts remain for
      their configured duration (15 minutes by default). Both are in memory,
      cleared on successful sign-in or restart.
    </li>
    <li>Scheduler tick records: 30 days.</li>
    <li>
      Account, work and credential records: until you or your operator delete
      them. Deleting a record leaves its change history unless your operator
      removes that too.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How it is protected</h2>
  <p>
    Public traffic is served over HTTPS with HSTS, a content security policy, and
    the usual browser hardening headers. Sign-in is rate limited and locks an
    account out after repeated failures. Stored credentials are encrypted.
    Tenants are separated by the API, which is the only client of the message
    broker. No system is perfect; report anything that looks wrong to the contact
    below.
  </p>

  <h2>Your choices</h2>
  <p>
    You can ask {@operator} for a copy of the data held about you, to correct it,
    or to delete your account and its records. Depending on where you live you may
    also have the right to object to processing or to complain to a data
    protection authority. Requests go to the contact below.
  </p>

  <h2>Children</h2>
  <p>
    The service is a tool for a working software crew. It is not directed at
    children and accounts are not knowingly created for them.
  </p>

  <h2>Changes</h2>
  <p>
    This policy changes when the software does. The date at the top of this page
    is the date the text last changed.
  </p>

  <h2>Contact</h2>
  <.contact email={@contact_email} operator={@operator} />
  <p>
    See also the <.link href={~p"/terms"}>Terms of Service</.link>.
  </p>
</Layouts.public>
